you've been critical at the decision not  to publish the obr reports alongside the  government Liz trouser said this morning  there are no plans to bring that  publication forward from when they are  currently at slated 23rd of November is  that good enough  no I don't think it is because right at  the heart of the problems we're  discussing here is a lack of confidence  that the markets have had in the fiscal  credibility of the government's policies  and the way that you establish that  fiscal credibility is you have firstly  credible fiscal targets and we don't yet  know what those are or when they're  going to bite secondly you have the obr  score all the measures including the  spending measures that you've probably  been talking about earlier in the  program and thirdly you have to have the  obr saying that all of that adds up to  hitting those particular targets now the  question then becomes when is the best  time to come forward with that obr  forecast now it's certainly the case you  can't prematurely Bring It Forward and  tomorrow morning would be too early for  example because it is an iterative  process and it takes time but I know  from my discussions with the obr that  they will be ready by the end of this  month to bring a full forecast forward  as opposed to the current date which is  the 23rd of November and why that's  important Sophie is for two reasons  firstly that the earlier you get that  out there assuming that all the numbers  Stack Up and the markets are comfortable  with it the sooner you can start to calm  those markets and the second reason is  that the monetary policy committee will  meet on the 3rd of November are you well  before the 23rd of November in order to  to take its decision on how high to  raise interest rates if it has a  satisfactory obr report before that  meeting on the 3rd of November I would  imagine and expect that the interest  rate rise will probably not be as high  as it otherwise would be and the reason  for that is that that obr report the  fiscal targets would have reassured the  markets there will be less concern about  the inflationary impacts of the  government's policy and therefore the  MPC will be putting interest rates up by  potentially and I do stress potentially  a little bit less and I say that because  these decisions are very finely balanced  the last NPC meeting for example the  vote was split three ways as to where  the movement and interest rates should  go so for stability reasons and keeping  it rates as low as we can and taking the  pressure off people with mortgages Etc  we need to have that obr forecast before  the end of this month  so just to be crystal clear the decision  to not bring forward the publication of  the obr that in your view is going to  directly hit people's mortgage mortgages  I use the word potentially because to  the extent that the lack of that  forecast means that there's greater  uncertainty in the markets which that  forecast could have calmed down and to  the extent that that in turn leads  members of the monetary policy committee  to conclude that they need to raise  rates a little bit higher than they  would otherwise had to have done then  yes there will be a direct knock-on cost  and that's why it is amongst other  reasons so important that that obr  forecast and the fiscal rules the  government's going to work to are  brought forward by the end of this month  now you've also written to the  chancellor asking him to give evidence  to the sac committee that your chair of  the treasury select committee as soon as  possible in the wake of the budget have  you got a reply yet  uh no my latest letter  um well if in fact we we got in touch  with uh the Chancellor's office a few  weeks ago we were told that he wouldn't  be in a position to appear before the  committee until after uh the uh medium  term fiscal plan is presented on the  23rd of November I wrote to him uh  earlier this week to say that I I needed  to hear from him t