 got to put  that in the context of an additional 72  billion pounds that's taking place as a  consequence of 45 billion pounds worth  of unfunded tax cuts that gives you a  sense of how far you've got to push in a  politically difficult Direction in order  to try and square the books here through  leaning into expenditure  I think it also gives us a sense of your  own views on it potentially as well Liz  trouser said this morning that she  didn't consult the cabinet on the  scrapping of the 45p rate of tax that  was a decision for the chancellor uh  quasi quateng Shu said do you think she  should have sought a bit more advice  I think she thought she has also said I  think that she feels that perhaps that  the ground should have been laid better  for the fiscal event last Friday and I  think I would put that in that category  in other words it appears to me that  this has been done certainly you know a  lot of stuff done at huge pays quite a  radical approach to institutions and  individuals and processes and the Norms  uh and amongst those various things yes  I think something like a move like that  you want to take your cabinet with you  you would want to discuss it with them  and I think that's typically how that  would have happened in the past  um Jake Bearer is also very clear that  as far as he's concerned if any  conservative MP votes against the budget  or the measures announced in the budget  that will be seen as a confidence Vape  so effectively they would have the whip  removed  do you think that's the right decision  that's uh always typically been the case  that if you vote against a major fiscal  measure in a finance bill then that is  considered a vote of uh confidence  against the government and the  consequence of that would be to have the  whip removed so I think if that is the  position that is adopted then I don't  think it'd be reasonable to say that  that's that's out of sync with uh with  what has happened in the past I think  the question really will be around the  Practical politics of that because  clearly if there are a number of back  benches or a Groundswell of resistance  to a particular measure  um then that would be problematic it is  a question of whether you can hold the  dam or not if you like uh on that and of  course sometimes these these pushes on  particular points can be resolved before  you even reach that point because the  government will on occasion amend the  proposals that it's putting forward none  of that of course on the 45p will come  in for some time because that will be in  a finance bill probably in the early  part of next year  at the same time though it's been pretty  clear from what uh Liz trust and quasi  quantity have said since the budget that  they have no intention of reversing the  45p measure given that how many of your  colleagues do you think might end up  voting against it  oh well I'm not going to get into  speculating around potential future  rebellions sometime uh next year as as I  said earlier I think the important thing  I'm sure that most iconies will be doing  this will be we want to know the full  extent of this package we want to know  where these spending uh cuts that the  government's now talking about are going  to fall and what they will mean and  indeed you know how they will impact  lower income families who who are as  always at times of of economic toil  generally the group that that suffer the  most so  um I think I and all my colleagues will  want to take a level-headed calm and  reasonable look at the entire package  that's on offer here  it doesn't sound like you are entirely  sure how you will be voting on some of  these measures I take the point that you  want to look at it in the round  but is that still  an open question  well I I don't know tremendously repeat  myself Sophie but this this is a very  big fiscal intervention it has many  moving Parts with huge consequences for  the economy and indeed for individuals  and families and businesses up and down  the country there's a lot happening and  I think it's only right particularly  when we get this obr forecast as I say  the earlier the better that we really  have a chance to look at that and look  at the detail and see how it all fits  together what the distributional impacts  are for example of all the measures that  are being undertaken and and quite  rightly the government is saying that  you know the the intervention on  supporting uh households or the with  their fuel bills will be  disproportionately beneficial to those  lower income households because they  have a higher proportion of their  expenditure uh that that's accounted for  by energy costs so there are swings and  roundabouts here and you have to look at  it in the round  uh briefly because I I'm out of time but  I want to squeeze one more in  um will Liz trust lead the conservative  party into the next election  sorry I slightly missed that when  so will Liz trance lead them I will  we'll trust yeah will she I I I  certainly hope so because  um for that to happen I think it's fair  to say that we have to fairly quickly  move to a place where the polls are  beginning to turn around where the  markets are feeling that the government  is behaving fiscally responsible and  they're gaining in confidence and the  pressure on the pound is being uh  released somewhat and bond yields are  not soaring off uh as as we see and if  that doesn't happen so then I Then I  then I think we are in hugely difficult  uh Waters
 

